# Difficult cards — what the 1997 game left out, and why we think so

A provenance note for the decklists, written 2026-09-07. Nothing in here
is a plan; it is the record of a mini research pass into the cards that
the 1997 MicroProse game is said to have shipped in its data files but
never turned on, so that when a community decklist names one of them
(Chaos Orb, above all) the substitution we make is documented rather
than quiet. The `.someday` suffix says the rest: a future pass may pick
any of these up, and this file is where it starts.

The rule of the file: every statement is either SOURCED (a quote, with
who said it, where and when) or a RECEIVED CLAIM (something we were
told, marked as such, not yet found in a readable source). The two are
kept apart on purpose — a decklist ledger is only worth having if it
says which of its reasons it can show.


## 1. Sources

| # | Where | Who / when | What it is |
|---|---|---|---|
| S1 | https://slightlymagic.net/forum/viewtopic.php?p=189014 | Korath (Manalink / Shandalar developer), dingbat1 — 21 Nov 2015 | "What card sets were in orginal Shandalar?" — the one first-hand account of the 1997 data files |
| S2 | https://slightlymagic.net/forum/viewtopic.php?f=85&t=1842&start=15 | joemuz, jatill (Manalink developer) — 16 Nov 2009 | The list of 1997-era cards still missing from Manalink in 2009, and a developer's view of which were codable |
| S3 | https://edhsilver.com/philosophy/ | EDH Silver, undated | General context on dexterity cards (Chaos Orb from Alpha, Falling Star from Legends) — says nothing about the 1997 game |
| S4 | https://www.reddit.com/r/Shandalar/comments/1oaa8ah/cool_as_f_shandalar_is_still_around/ | r/Shandalar thread | Could not be read from this machine (only the page shell came back, no comments). Everything attributed to it below stays a received claim |

Not consulted: the decompiled 1997 sources (the `benprew/microprose-shandalar-source`
project) are not on this machine, and `../shandalar-src/` is a Manalink 3
(2016) tree — its `Info.csv` / `Legacy.csv` / `Concise.csv` are Manalink's
own card database, evidence of what Manalink implemented, not of what
1997 shipped. Two of its rows are worth keeping in mind all the same:
`Chaos Orb … "Silly Orb Thing"` (id 1002) and `Nevinyrral's Disk …
"History Eraser Button"` (id 1040) — Manalink's later, software-only
Orb, and the Disk that this repository swaps in for it.


## 2. What the 1997 game had (sourced)

Korath, S1, 21 Nov 2015 — the pool:

> all of Fourth Edition; most of Unlimited, Arabian Nights, Antiquities,
> The Dark; bits of Legends; around six promo cards (Arena, Giant Badger …);
> the twelve Astral cards; nothing from Fallen Empires.

(Paraphrased from the post; the set list matches `tools/fetch_cards.py`'s
eight Scryfall sets — 2ed, 4ed, arn, atq, past, phpr, leg, drk — and the
Astral twelve under `cards/sets/past/`.)

Korath, S1, same post — the cards in the files but not in the game:

> The only cards I've found that were implemented but "not turned on"
> … are Chaos Orb, City in a Bottle, Cocoon, Golgothian Sylex,
> Metamorphosis, Power Artifact, Transmutation, and Wood Elemental, all
> of which are broken to varying extent.

> There's more cards, including plenty of gold ones, that had entries
> defined in cards1.dat, but that doesn't contain much more than card
> text, frame, power, toughness, and mana cost — only enough to display
> the card on the screen. That's not "implemented" by any reasonable
> measure.

dingbat1, S1, 21 Nov 2015:

> the cards were implemented, but not turned on. With very minor
> modification, the cards could be accessed in the deckbuilder

> Notably, it did not have any gold cards.

That is the whole of the first-hand evidence: EIGHT cards with real code
behind them that the shipped game hid, "broken to varying extent", and
an unknown number of text-only stubs (many of them gold / Legends
multicolour cards) that never had rules code at all.


## 3. The eight "implemented but not turned on" cards

For each: what the sources support, what we were told (received claim),
what this repository does today, and what a future pass would need.

### Chaos Orb (Alpha / Unlimited, artifact — the flip)

- SOURCED: in the 1997 data files, hidden, broken (Korath, S1). Still
  missing from Manalink in 2009 (joemuz, S2). A dexterity card by
  design (S3).
- RECEIVED CLAIM: that it was left out because the physical flipping
  mechanic has no honest software form. Plausible on its face — S3
  gives the general reason dexterity cards are banned everywhere — but
  no source ties that reasoning to MicroProse's decision.
- HERE: excluded from the pool outright, `tools/fetch_cards.py`
  `EXCLUDED_NAMES`, following s30's precedent (`docs/audit-vs-s30.md`,
  "DEFAULT_EXCLUDED_NAMES"). Not a stub, not simplified: absent.
- DECKLIST CONSEQUENCE: the community lists in `docs/decks-1997.md`
  count Chaos Orb as their most-proxied name (tables at lines ~477,
  ~657, ~706; "The Old School lists proxy mostly one name, Chaos Orb,
  which the pool does not hold"). `decks/variants/the_deck_playable.deck`
  is The Deck with Nevinyrral's Disk in the Orb's slot — the header of
  that file explains that this is the one change and why it is filed
  under "Playable variants" rather than with the ledgers.
- SOMEDAY: a software Orb. Manalink's precedent is the "Silly Orb Thing"
  — {1}, tap and sacrifice, destroy target nontoken permanent — i.e. a
  guaranteed hit with no flip; a stricter reading would be a coin flip
  per attempt. Either is a `[QoL]` invention, which is why it is not
  done: the 1997 game did not have it either.

### City in a Bottle (Arabian Nights, artifact)

- SOURCED: in the files, hidden, broken (Korath, S1); missing from
  Manalink in 2009 (joemuz, S2); jatill (S2) thought it "should be
  doable".
- RECEIVED CLAIM: that Wizards of the Coast ordered MicroProse to cut it,
  and that it "ruined deck diversity" (attributed to S4, unreadable
  here). NOT FOUND in any source we could read; Korath's account gives
  no reason at all for any of the eight. Treat as folklore until
  someone quotes the thread.
- HERE: implemented, `cards/sets/arn/city_in_a_bottle.gd` — the
  play-ban keyed on the card NAME through
  `CardRegistry.originally_printed_in(name, "arn")`, so a Fourth
  Edition reprint of an Arabian Nights card is still an Arabian Nights
  card, as printed.

### Golgothian Sylex (Antiquities, artifact)

- SOURCED: in the files, hidden, broken (Korath, S1); missing from
  Manalink in 2009 (joemuz, S2); jatill (S2) thought it doable.
- RECEIVED CLAIM: "the Antiquities equivalent, disabled for the same
  reason" — the same-reason half is unsourced (see City in a Bottle).
- HERE: implemented, `cards/sets/atq/golgothian_sylex.gd`, the same
  originally-printed-in test against `atq`.

### Wood Elemental (Legends, creature — sacrifice untapped Forests as it enters)

- SOURCED: in the files, hidden, broken (Korath, S1).
- RECEIVED CLAIM: that the "sacrifice any number of untapped Forests"
  choice was error-prone in the 1997 engine. Unsourced; nothing in S1
  says which way any of the eight was broken.
- HERE: implemented, `cards/sets/leg/wood_elemental.gd`, through the
  ordinary `choose_number` funnel.

### Cocoon (Legends, enchantment — pupa counters)

- SOURCED: in the files, hidden, broken (Korath, S1).
- RECEIVED CLAIM: that tracking the pupa counters across upkeeps broke.
  Unsourced.
- HERE: implemented, `cards/sets/leg/cocoon.gd`.

### Metamorphosis (Arabian Nights, sorcery — sacrifice a creature for mana)

- SOURCED: in the files, hidden, broken (Korath, S1); missing from
  Manalink in 2009 (joemuz, S2).
- RECEIVED CLAIM: that the AI failed the sacrifice-then-spend sequence.
  Unsourced.
- HERE: implemented, `cards/sets/arn/metamorphosis.gd`.

### Power Artifact (Antiquities, enchant artifact — cost reduction)

- SOURCED: in the files, hidden, broken (Korath, S1).
- RECEIVED CLAIM: that dynamic activation-cost reductions did not fit
  the 1997 cost model. Unsourced — though of the eight it is the claim
  that best matches the shape of the card.
- HERE: implemented, `cards/sets/atq/power_artifact.gd`.

### Transmutation (Legends, instant — swap power and toughness)

- SOURCED: in the files, hidden, broken (Korath, S1).
- RECEIVED CLAIM: that a P/T swap fought the engine's continuous-effect
  layering. Unsourced.
- HERE: implemented, `cards/sets/leg/transmutation.gd`, in the layer
  system (`docs/mechanics.md`, layers).

Summary: of the eight, SEVEN are in this repository's pool and
implemented; ONE (Chaos Orb) is deliberately absent. No decklist in
`decks/` needs any of the seven proxied.


## 4. The 2009 Manalink "still missing" list (sourced, S2)

joemuz's list of 16 Nov 2009 — cards from the 1997-era sets that
Manalink had not implemented by then. Useful here because Manalink began
from the 1997 code, so a card missing in 2009 is a card the 1997 engine
gave them nothing for. It is NOT a list of what 1997 lacked (Illusionary
Mask, for one, is in the 1997 pool).

- Alpha/Beta/Unlimited (5): Word of Command, Camouflage, False Orders,
  Chaos Orb, Illusionary Mask.
- Arabian Nights (3): Metamorphosis, Shahrazad, City in a Bottle.
- Antiquities (1): Golgothian Sylex.
- The Dark (40): a set-sized gap — Manalink had barely started The Dark
  in 2009. (The full forty are in the thread; they are ordinary cards
  and all in our pool through the `drk` set.)

jatill, S2, 16 Nov 2009, on the A/B/U five:

> I guessing they are mostly uncodable, though (ex: Illusionary Mask)

and on the rest:

> looking through the list of old cards that were left out, I believe
> the City in A Gottle and Gothian Cyclex should be doable.

(Spelling as in the post.)

HERE: Camouflage (`cards/sets/2ed/camouflage.gd`), False Orders
(`cards/sets/2ed/false_orders.gd`) and Illusionary Mask
(`cards/sets/2ed/illusionary_mask.gd`, real face-down permanents; its
remaining deviation is listed in `docs/simplified-cards.md`) are
implemented. Word of Command and Shahrazad are excluded (section 6).


## 5. The text-only stubs (sourced, S1)

Korath's second paragraph in section 2 is the whole of it: cards1.dat
carried entries — text, frame, P/T, cost, "only enough to display the
card on the screen" — for cards the game had no rules for, "plenty of
gold ones" among them. dingbat1 adds that the shipped game had no gold
cards at all. These were templates, not cards, and nothing in the 1997
game or in any decklist ledger depends on them.

RECEIVED CLAIM: "dozens of gold/Legends cards existed as empty
templates". The number is unsourced; Korath says "plenty". Our pool
takes Legends whole from Scryfall (`leg`, all colours), so the gold
cards this game never played are playable here — an `[s30]`-style
widening, noted in `docs/decks-1997.md`, not a fidelity claim.

`cards/data/dck_ids.txt` (371 name→id pairs harvested from original
`.dck` files) is the one 1997-side witness this repository holds, and it
is silent on all of the above: none of the eight, nor Shahrazad, Falling
Star, Word of Command, Camouflage or False Orders appear in it, while
Nevinyrral's Disk does (id 173). A card that no shipped deck could
contain leaves no id in the deck files — consistent with Korath, and
nothing more.


## 6. Our own exclusions, for the record

`tools/fetch_cards.py`, `EXCLUDED_NAMES = {"Chaos Orb", "Shahrazad",
"Word of Command", "Falling Star"}` — the same three as s30 plus Falling
Star (the Legends dexterity card, S3), see `docs/mechanics.md` ("excluded
from the pool outright … not simplified") and `docs/audit-vs-s30.md`.

| Card | Why it is out | Someday |
|---|---|---|
| Chaos Orb | Dexterity — the flip has no honest software form | A software Orb (Manalink's "Silly Orb Thing": {1}, T, sacrifice: destroy target nontoken permanent — or a coin flip). A `[QoL]` invention either way |
| Falling Star | Dexterity, same as the Orb | Same — a random-or-guaranteed 3 damage to a set of creatures |
| Shahrazad | A subgame: the duel inside the duel | Needs a second `MtgGame` on the same seats with half-life stakes; the engine is a plain object and could host one, the screen could not, today |
| Word of Command | Drives the opponent's turn: choose a card in their hand and make them play it | A bounded form fits the owner's 2026-09-07 ruling (a list from the opponent's hand, the engine casts it), but it still needs "play it as if you controlled them" |

Also open, though in the pool: Illusionary Mask's remaining deviation
(`docs/simplified-cards.md`), and Magical Hack / Sleight of Mind, whose
word lists follow the owner's ruling of 2026-09-07 (the lists the engine
models, no free text).


## 7. What a future pass would need

1. Read S4 from a machine that can, and quote it — the WotC-ordered-cut
   story for City in a Bottle is the one claim above that would change
   the ledger's wording if it held.
2. Read the decompiled 1997 sources for the eight: which way each was
   broken, and whether any was cut by a flag or by missing code. That
   turns every RECEIVED CLAIM in section 3 into a sourced line or
   strikes it.
3. Decide the Orb. Until then The Deck is played with the Disk, and the
   community lists that name the Orb stay proxied in the ledger.
